./ralph/codex-output/iteration-000031-20260518-225437.log:2252:ralph/progress.txt:19920:  - **Mutating-verb framing for CapSupport actions:** admin.break_glass is a mutating verb (creates an elevated session) and admin.reconcile is a mutating verb (issues Dokploy writes), but BOTH are catalogued as CapSupport because the CAPABILITY captures "actions only Yalla support should be able to perform across the tenant boundary" — not "read vs write". The engine's cross-tenant exception (`required == CapRead || CapSupport`) deliberately admits CapSupport regardless of read/write nature. This is the load-bearing distinction from CapDeploy verbs (job.retry, job.cancel) where Support is denied cross-tenant. When a future admin verb lands (e.g. admin.rotate_root_credentials, admin.purge_orphan_dokploy), the question to ask is "does Yalla support need to be able to do this cross-tenant during incident response?" — if yes, CapSupport; if no (e.g. admin actions that should remain inside the tenant's own Owner authority), a different cap class.
