+## 2026-05-19 18:57 +03 - BE-0553
+- What was implemented
+  - Completed the incident response documentation story by extending `docs/operations/incident-response.md` with expected output shapes for Go gates, health/readiness/version envelopes, structured log evidence, and `dead_letter_alerts` evidence.
+  - Added a focused static assertion to `internal/release/incident_response_runbook_static_test.go` so the expected-output guidance stays pinned.
+  - Updated `SECURITY.md`, `internal/release/AGENTS.md`, and the Codebase Patterns section with the reusable incident-response evidence pattern.
+  - Marked BE-0553 as passing in `ralph/prd.json`.
+- Files changed
+  - `docs/operations/incident-response.md`
+  - `internal/release/incident_response_runbook_static_test.go`
+  - `SECURITY.md`
+  - `internal/release/AGENTS.md`
+  - `ralph/prd.json`
+  - `ralph/progress.txt`
+- Verification run
+  - RED: `go test ./internal/release/... -run TestIncidentResponseRunbookArtifactDocumentsExpectedOutputs` failed before the runbook update because `## Expected outputs` was missing.
+  - `go test ./internal/release/... -run TestIncidentResponseRunbookArtifact`
+  - `gofmt -w .`
+  - `goimports -w .` was not run because `goimports` is not installed in this environment.
+  - `go mod tidy`
+  - `go test ./...`
+  - `go test -race ./...`
+  - `go vet ./...`
+  - `golangci-lint run ./...`
+  - `staticcheck ./...` was not run because `staticcheck` is not installed in this environment.
+  - `govulncheck ./...` was not run because `govulncheck` is not installed in this environment.
+- Results
+  - Required checks passed: focused incident-response artifact test, formatting, module cleanup, full test suite, race detector, and vet.
+  - `golangci-lint run ./...` passed with 0 issues.
+- Remaining risks or follow-ups
+  - No runtime API, worker, persistence, policy, quota, or Dokploy behavior changed in this documentation-only story.
+  - Remaining documentation stories after BE-0553 are still marked `passes: false`.
+- **Learnings for future iterations:**
+  - Incident-response documentation should record stable evidence shapes instead of full transcripts: command names, target environment, timestamp, incident ID, package-level `ok` / `PASS`, envelope examples, structured-log key presence, and metric section names.
+  - Static documentation tests depend on exact substrings; keep load-bearing phrases on one line when pinning them.
+---
 - Worker provisioning-job outcome writes distinguish cancelled jobs from stale lease ownership: `JobRepository.Transition` returns `apierr.JobCancelled()` / `E_JOB_CANCELLED` when an expected-lease outcome targets a job already moved to `cancelled`, while lease-owner mismatch on an active/reclaimed job remains `apierr.JobNotClaimed()` / `E_JOB_NOT_CLAIMED`.
 - Provisioning job outcome writes should prove lease ownership at commit time: `worker.StoreClaimer.complete` sets `store.JobTransition.ExpectedLeaseOwner`, and `JobRepository.Transition` returns typed `apierr.JobNotClaimed()` / `E_JOB_NOT_CLAIMED` when the locked row is no longer leased by that worker. This prevents stale workers from overwriting a job already reclaimed after lease expiry.
 - Reconciliation-service-type stories should pin the subtype with a focused `internal/controlplane/reconcile/*_test.go` matrix while updating the canonical closed-set table when a new `ActionType` / `DriftReason` is introduced. Build/source config drift is value-bearing like env vars: route desired values only through Repairer-only fields (`Action.DesiredBuild`), keep `Type`/`Kind`/`Reason`/IDs value-free, and normalize build settings before diffing so renderer defaults (blank builder => dockerfile, blank dockerfile path => `Dockerfile`) do not create false positives.

